                "senderName": "NWS Phoenix AZ",
                "headline": "Flash Flood Warning issued October 11 at 11:04AM MST until October 11 at 1:15PM MST by NWS Phoenix AZ",
                "description": "FFWPSR\n\nThe National Weather Service in Phoenix has extended the\n\n* Flash Flood Warning for...\nThe Sand Stone and Spring burn scar in...\nGila County in east central Arizona...\nMaricopa County in south central Arizona...\n\n* Until 115 PM MST.\n\n* At 1104 AM MST, Doppler radar and automated rain gauges indicated\nthunderstorms producing heavy rain over the Sand Stone and Spring\nBurn Scars, as well as across portions of northeast Maricopa\nCounty. Between 0.75 and 2 inches of rain have fallen. Flash\nflooding is ongoing or expected to begin shortly. Excessive\nrainfall over the burn scar will result in debris flow. The debris\nflow can consist of rock, mud, vegetation and other loose\nmaterials.\n\nHAZARD...Life threatening flash flooding. Thunderstorms producing\nflash flooding in and around the Sand Stone and Spring\nBurn Scar.\n\nSOURCE...Radar and automated gauges.\n\nIMPACT...Life threatening flash flooding of areas in and around the\nSand Stone and Spring Burn Scar.\n\n* Some locations that will experience flash flooding include...\nBallantine Trailhead, Round Valley, Sugarloaf Mountain, Sunflower,\nSycamore Creek, Saguaro Lake and Salt River Tubing Recreation Area.\n\nThis includes AZ Route 87 between mile markers 196 and 205.",
                "instruction": "This is a life threatening situation. Heavy rainfall will cause\nextensive and severe flash flooding of creeks...streams...and ditches\nin the Sand Stone and Spring Burn Scar. Severe debris flows can also\nbe anticipated across roads. Roads and driveways may be washed away\nin places. If you encounter flood waters...climb to safety.\n\nMove away from recently burned areas. Life-threatening flooding of\ncreeks, roads and normally dry arroyos is likely. The heavy rains\nwill likely trigger rockslides, mudslides and debris flows in steep\nterrain, especially in and around these areas.\n\nTurn around, don't drown when encountering flooded roads. Most flood\ndeaths occur in vehicles.\n\nIn hilly terrain there are hundreds of low water crossings which are\npotentially dangerous in heavy rain. Do not attempt to cross flooded\nroads. Find an alternate route.",
                "response": "Avoid",
                "note": null,
